#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE EIGHTH STAGE

When they had gone from the shepherds, they quickly came to the place where Christian met one Turn-away, who lived in the town of Apostasy. So Mr. Great-Heart their guide now reminded them of him, saying, This is the place where Christian met one Turn-away, who carried with him the mark of his rebellion on his back. And this I have to say concerning this man: he would listen to no counsel, but once he was falling, persuasion could not stop him. When he came to the place where the cross and the sepulcher were, he met one who told him to look there; but he gnashed with his teeth, and stamped, and said he was resolved to go back to his own town. Before he came to the gate, he met Evangelist, who offered to lay hands on him, to turn him into the way again; but this Turn-away resisted him, and having treated him with much contempt, he got away over the wall, and so escaped his hand.

Then they went on; and just at the place where Little-Faith was robbed before, there stood a man with his sword drawn, and his face all over with blood. Then said Mr. Great-Heart, Who are you? The man answered, saying, I am one whose name is Valiant-for-truth. I am a pilgrim, and am going to the Celestial City. Now, as I was on my way, there were three men who beset me, and proposed to me these three things: 1. Whether I would become one of them. 2. Or go back to where I came from. 3. Or die on the spot. Prov 1:11–14. To the first I answered, I had been an honest man for a long time, and therefore it could not be expected that I should now cast in my lot with thieves. Then they demanded what I would say to the second. So I told them that, as for the place I came from, if I had not found it a hardship to me, I would not have forsaken it at all; but finding it altogether unsuitable to me, and very unprofitable for me, I forsook it for this way. Then they asked me what I said to the third. And I told them my life cost far too dear for me to give it away lightly. Besides, you have no business putting things to my choice in this way; so be it at your peril if you meddle. Then these three, that is, Wild-head, Inconsiderate, and Pragmatic, drew on me, and I also drew on them. So we fell to it, one against three, for more than three hours. They have left upon me, as you see, some of the marks of their valor, and have also carried away with them some of mine. They have only just now gone; I suppose they might, as the saying is, have heard your horse dash, and so they took to flight.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But here were great odds, three against one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It is true; but few and many are nothing to him who has the truth on his side. “Though an army encamps around me,” said one, Ps 27:3, “my heart will not fear; though a war breaks out against me, I will keep my trust,” etc. Besides, said he, I have read in some records that one man has fought an army: and how many did Samson slay with the jawbone of an ass!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said the guide, Why did you not cry out, so that some might have come in to help you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: So I did, to my King, who I knew could hear me, and give invisible help, and that was sufficient for me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then said Great-Heart to Mr. Valiant-for-truth, You have behaved worthily; let me see your sword. So he showed it to him.]

When he had taken it in his hand, and looked at it a while, he said, Ha, it is a true Jerusalem blade.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It is so. Let a man have one of these blades, with a hand to wield it and skill to use it, and he may venture upon an angel with it. He need not fear its holding, if only he knows how to lay on. Its edge will never blunt. It will cut flesh and bones, and soul, and spirit, and all. Heb 4:12.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But you fought a great while; I wonder you were not weary.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: I fought until my sword cleaved to my hand; and then they were joined together as if a sword grew out of my arm; and when the blood ran through my fingers, then I fought with most courage.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You have done well; you have resisted unto blood, striving against sin. You shall stay with us, come in and go out with us; for we are your companions. Then they took him and washed his wounds, and gave him some of what they had, to refresh him; and so they went on together.]

Now, as they went on, because Mr. Great-Heart was delighted in him (for he greatly loved one whom he found to be a man of valor), and because there were those in the company who were feeble and weak, he therefore questioned him about many things; first, what countryman he was.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: I am of Dark-land; for there I was born, and there my father and mother are still.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Dark-land! said the guide; does that not lie on the same coast as the City of Destruction?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes, it does. Now what caused me to come on pilgrimage was this. We had one Mr. Tell-true come into our parts, and he spread it about what Christian had done, who went from the City of Destruction; namely, how he had forsaken his wife and children, and had taken up a pilgrim’s life. It was also confidently reported how he had killed a serpent that came out to resist him in his journey, and how he got through to where he intended. It was also told what welcome he had at all his Lord’s lodgings, especially when he came to the gates of the Celestial City; for there, said the man, he was received with the sound of trumpets by a company of shining ones. He told also how all the bells in the city rang for joy at his reception, and what golden garments he was clothed with; with many other things that I shall now refrain from relating. In a word, that man so told the story of Christian and his travels that my heart fell into a burning haste to be gone after him; nor could father or mother hold me back. So I got away from them, and have come this far on my way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You came in at the gate, did you not?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes, yes; for the same man also told us that all would be nothing if we did not begin to enter this way at the gate.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Look, said the guide to Christiana, the pilgrimage of your husband, and what he has gained by it, is spread abroad far and near.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, is this Christian’s wife?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, that it is; and these also are his four sons.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: What, and going on pilgrimage too?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, truly, they are following after.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It gladdens me at the heart. Good man, how joyful he will be when he sees those who would not go with him, yet entering after him in at the gates into the Celestial City!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Without doubt it will be a comfort to him; for, next to the joy of seeing himself there, it will be a joy to meet his wife and children there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: But now that you are on that, please let me hear your opinion about it. Some question whether we shall know one another when we are there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Do you think they will know themselves then, or that they will rejoice to see themselves in that bliss? And if they think they will know themselves and do this, why not know others, and rejoice in their welfare also? Again, since our relatives are our second self, though that state will be dissolved there, yet why may it not be reasonably concluded that we shall be more glad to see them there than to find them missing?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Well, I see where you stand on this. Have you any more things to ask me about my beginning to come on pilgrimage?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes; were your father and mother willing that you should become a pilgrim?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: O no; they used every means imaginable to persuade me to stay at home.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Why, what could they say against it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They said it was an idle life; and if I myself were not inclined to sloth and laziness, I would never favor a pilgrim’s condition.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And what else did they say?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, they told me that it was a dangerous way; indeed, the most dangerous way in the world, they said, is the one the pilgrims go.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did they show you in what respect this way is so dangerous?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes; and that in many particulars.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Name some of them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They told me of the Slough of Despond, where Christian was nearly smothered. They told me that there were archers standing ready in Beelzebub-castle to shoot those who should knock at the Wicket-gate for entrance. They told me also of the wood and dark mountains; of the hill Difficulty; of the lions; and also of the three giants, Bloody-man, Maul, and Slay-good. They said, moreover, that there was a foul fiend that haunted the Valley of Humiliation, and that Christian was almost robbed of his life by him. Besides, they said, you must go over the Valley of the Shadow of Death, where the hobgoblins are, where the light is darkness, where the way is full of snares, pits, traps, and nooses. They told me also of Giant Despair, of Doubting Castle, and of the ruin that the pilgrims met with here. Further, they said I must go over the Enchanted Ground, which was dangerous; and that after all this I should find a river, over which there was no bridge; and that that river lay between me and the Celestial country.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And was this all?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No. They also told me that this way was full of deceivers, and of persons who lay in wait there to turn good men out of the path.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: But how did they make that out?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: They told me that Mr. Worldly Wiseman lay there in wait to deceive. They said also that there were Formality and Hypocrisy continually on the road. They said also that By-ends, Talkative, or Demas would be likely to gather me up; that the Flatterer would catch me in his net; or that, with green-headed Ignorance, I would presume to go on to the gate, from where he was sent back to the hole that was in the side of the hill, and made to go the by-way to hell.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: I promise you, this was enough to discourage you; but did they make an end there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No, wait. They told me also of many who had tried that way of old, and who had gone a great way in it, to see if they could find something of the glory there that so many had talked so much of from time to time; and how they came back again, and called themselves fools for setting a foot out of doors on that path, to the satisfaction of all the country. And they named several who did so, such as Obstinate and Pliable, Mistrust and Timorous, Turn-away and old Atheist, with several more; some of whom, they said, had gone far to see what they could find, but not one of them had found as much advantage by going as amounted to the weight of a feather.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Did they say anything more to discourage you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Yes. They told me of one Mr. Fearing, who was a pilgrim, and how he found his way so solitary that he never had a comfortable hour in it; also that Mr. Despondency had nearly starved in it; yes, and also (which I had almost forgotten) that Christian himself, about whom there has been such a noise, after all his adventures for a celestial crown, was certainly drowned in the Black River, and never went a foot further, however it was hushed up.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: And did none of these things discourage you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: No; they seemed only so many nothings to me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: How did that come about?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Why, I still believed what Mr. Tell-true had said; and that carried me beyond them all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Then this was your victory, even your faith.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: It was so. I believed, and therefore came out, got into the way, fought all who set themselves against me, and, by believing, have come to this place.]

#quote[“Who would true valor see, \
Let him come hither; \
One here will constant be, \
Come wind, come weather; \
There’s no discouragement \
Shall make him once relent \
His first avow’d intent \
To be a pilgrim.]

#quote[Whoso beset him round \
With dismal stories, \
Do but themselves confound; \
His strength the more is. \
No lion can him fright, \
He’ll with a giant fight, \
But he will have a right \
To be a pilgrim.]

#quote[Hobgoblin nor foul fiend \
Can daunt his spirit; \
He knows he at the end \
Shall life inherit. \
Then fancies fly away, \
He’ll not fear what men say; \
He’ll labor night and day \
To be a pilgrim.”]

By this time they had come to the Enchanted Ground, where the air naturally tended to make one drowsy. And that place was all grown over with briars and thorns, except here and there, where there was an enchanted arbor, on which if a man sits, or in which if a man sleeps, it is a question, some say, whether he will ever rise or wake again in this world. Over this forest, therefore, they went, both one and another, and Mr. Great-Heart went before, because he was the guide; and Mr. Valiant-for-truth came behind, as rear-guard, for fear lest perhaps some fiend, or dragon, or giant, or thief should fall upon their rear, and so do mischief. They went on here, each man with his sword drawn in his hand; for they knew it was a dangerous place. Also they cheered one another up as well as they could. Mr. Great-Heart commanded that Feeble-mind should come up after him; and Mr. Despondency was under the eye of Mr. Valiant.

Now they had not gone far when a great mist and darkness fell upon them all, so that for a great while they could scarcely see one another. So they were forced, for some time, to feel for one another by words; for they did not walk by sight. But anyone must think that here was only hard going for the best of them all; but how much worse for the women and children, who were tender both of feet and of heart! Yet so it was that, through the encouraging words of him who led in the front, and of him who brought them up behind, they managed pretty well to jog along.

The way here was also very wearisome, through dirt and sludge. Nor was there, on all this ground, so much as one inn or eating-house in which to refresh the feebler sort. Here, therefore, was grunting, and puffing, and sighing, while one tumbles over a bush, another sticks fast in the dirt, and the children, some of them, lost their shoes in the mire; while one cries out, I am down; and another, Ho, where are you? and a third, The bushes have got such fast hold on me, I think I cannot get away from them.

Then they came to an arbor, warm, and promising much refreshment to the pilgrims; for it was finely made overhead, beautified with greenery, and furnished with benches and seats. It also had in it a soft couch on which the weary might lean. This, you must think, all things considered, was tempting; for the pilgrims were already beginning to be worn down by the badness of the way: but there was not one of them who made so much as a motion to stop there. Indeed, for all I could see, they continually paid such good heed to the advice of their guide, and he so faithfully told them of dangers, and of the nature of the dangers when they were at them, that usually, when they were nearest to them, they plucked up their spirits most, and heartened one another to deny the flesh. This arbor was called The Slothful’s Friend, and was made on purpose to allure, if it might, some of the pilgrims to take up their rest there when weary.

I saw then in my dream that they went on in this their solitary ground, till they came to a place at which a man is apt to lose his way. Now, though when it was light their guide could tell well enough how to avoid the ways that led wrong, yet in the dark he was brought to a standstill. But he had in his pocket a map of all ways leading to or from the Celestial City; so he struck a light (for he never goes without his tinder-box also), and takes a look at his book or map, which bids him be careful in that place to turn to the right hand. And had he not been careful here to look at his map, they would all, in all probability, have been smothered in the mud; for just a little before them, and that at the end of the cleanest way too, was a pit, no one knows how deep, full of nothing but mud, made there on purpose to destroy the pilgrims in.

Then I thought to myself, Who that goes on pilgrimage would not want one of these maps with him, so that he may look, when he is at a standstill, which is the way he must take?

Then they went on in this Enchanted Ground till they came to where there was another arbor, and it was built by the highway-side. And in that arbor there lay two men, whose names were Heedless and Too-bold. These two went this far on pilgrimage; but here, being wearied with their journey, they sat down to rest themselves, and so fell fast asleep. When the pilgrims saw them, they stood still and shook their heads; for they knew that the sleepers were in a pitiful case. Then they consulted what to do, whether to go on and leave them in their sleep, or to step over to them and try to wake them; so they concluded to go to them and wake them, that is, if they could; but with this caution, namely, to take heed that they themselves did not sit down nor embrace the offered benefit of that arbor.

So they went in, and spoke to the men, and called each by his name, for the guide, it seems, knew them; but there was no voice nor answer. Then the guide shook them, and did what he could to rouse them. Then one of them said, I will pay you when I get my money. At which the guide shook his head. I will fight as long as I can hold my sword in my hand, said the other. At that, one of the children laughed.

Then Christiana said, What is the meaning of this? The guide said, They are talking in their sleep. If you strike them, beat them, or whatever else you do to them, they will answer you in this fashion; or, as one of them said in old time, when the waves of the sea beat upon him, and he slept as one on the mast of a ship, Prov 23:34, 35, When I awake, I will seek it yet again. You know, when men talk in their sleep, they say anything; but their words are not governed either by faith or reason. There is an incoherence in their words now, as there was before between their going on pilgrimage and sitting down here. This, then, is the mischief of it: when heedless ones go on pilgrimage, it is twenty to one that they are served like this. For this Enchanted Ground is one of the last refuges that the enemy of pilgrims has; so it is, as you see, placed almost at the end of the way, and so it stands against us with the more advantage. For when, thinks the enemy, will these fools be so desirous to sit down as when they are weary? and when so likely to be weary as when almost at their journey’s end? That is why, I say, the Enchanted Ground is placed so close to the land Beulah, and so near the end of their race. So let pilgrims look to themselves, lest it happen to them as it has to these who, as you see, have fallen asleep, and no one can wake them.

Then the pilgrims desired with trembling to go forward; only they begged their guide to strike a light, that they might go the rest of their way by the help of the light of a lantern. So he struck a light, and they went by the help of that through the rest of this way, though the darkness was very great. 2 Pet 1:19. But the children began to be sorely weary, and they cried out to him who loves pilgrims to make their way more comfortable. So by the time they had gone a little further, a wind arose that drove away the fog, so the air became clearer. Yet they were not off the Enchanted Ground (by a long way); only now they could see one another better, and the way in which they should walk.

Now when they were almost at the end of this ground, they perceived that a little before them there was a solemn noise, as of one who was much concerned. So they went on and looked before them: and behold, they saw, as they thought, a man on his knees, with hands and eyes lifted up, and speaking, as they thought, earnestly to one who was above. They drew near, but could not tell what he said; so they went softly till he had done. When he had done, he got up, and began to run toward the Celestial City. Then Mr. Great-Heart called after him, saying, Ho there, friend, let us have your company, if you are going, as I suppose you are, to the Celestial City. So the man stopped, and they came up to him. But as soon as Mr. Honest saw him, he said, I know this man. Then Mr. Valiant-for-truth said, Please, who is it? It is one, said he, who comes from near where I lived. His name is Standfast; he is certainly a right good pilgrim.

So they came up to one another; and at once Standfast said to old Honest, Ho, father Honest, are you there? Aye, said he, that I am, as sure as you are there. Right glad am I, said Mr. Standfast, that I have found you on this road. And as glad am I, said the other, that I caught sight of you on your knees. Then Mr. Standfast blushed, and said, But why, did you see me? Yes, that I did, said the other, and my heart was glad at the sight. Why, what did you think? said Standfast. Think! said old Honest; what could I think? I thought we had an honest man on the road, and therefore should have his company by and by. If you thought rightly, said Standfast, how happy I am! But if I am not as I should be, it is I alone who must bear it. That is true, said the other; but your fear further confirms me that things are right between the Prince of pilgrims and your soul. For he says, “Blessed is the man who fears always.” Prov 28:14.

#speech[#smallcaps[Valiant-for-Truth]: Well, but, brother, please tell us what was the cause of your being on your knees just now: was it because some special mercy laid obligations upon you, or what?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Why, we are, as you see, on the Enchanted Ground; and as I was coming along, I was musing with myself on how dangerous the road in this place was, and how many who had come even this far on pilgrimage had here been stopped and destroyed. I thought also of the manner of death with which this place destroys men. Those who die here die of no violent disease: the death which such die is not grievous to them. For he who goes away in a sleep begins that journey with desire and pleasure. Indeed, such acquiesce in the will of that disease.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then Mr. Honest, interrupting him, said, Did you see the two men asleep in the arbor?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Yes, yes, I saw Heedless and Too-bold there; and for all I know, there they will lie till they rot. Prov 10:7. But let me go on with my tale. As I was musing like this, as I said, there was one in very pleasant attire, but old, who presented herself to me, and offered me three things, namely, her body, her purse, and her bed. Now the truth is, I was both weary and sleepy. I am also as poor as an owlet, and perhaps the witch knew that. Well, I rebuffed her again and again, but she brushed my refusals aside, and smiled. Then I began to be angry; but she did not mind that at all. Then she made offers again, and said that if I would be ruled by her, she would make me great and happy; for, said she, I am the mistress of the world, and men are made happy by me. Then I asked her name, and she told me it was Madam Bubble. This set me further from her; but she still followed me with enticements. Then I took myself, as you saw, to my knees, and with hands lifted up, and cries, I prayed to Him who had said he would help. So, just as you came up, the gentlewoman went her way. Then I continued to give thanks for this my great deliverance; for I truly believe she intended no good, but rather sought to stop me in my journey.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Without doubt her designs were bad. But wait, now that you talk of her, I think I have either seen her, or have read some story of her.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Perhaps you have done both.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Madam Bubble! Is she not a tall, comely lady, somewhat swarthy in complexion?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Right, you hit it: she is just such a one.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Does she not speak very smoothly, and give you a smile at the end of a sentence?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: You hit upon it again, for these are her very actions.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Does she not wear a great purse by her side, and is her hand not often in it, fingering her money, as if that were her heart’s delight?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: It is just so; had she stood by all this while, you could not have set her forth before me more fully, nor have better described her features.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Honest]: Then he who drew her picture was a good painter, and he who wrote of her spoke truly.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: This woman is a witch, and it is by virtue of her sorceries that this ground is enchanted. Whoever lays his head down in her lap might as well lay it down on that block over which the axe hangs; and whoever lay their eyes upon her beauty are counted the enemies of God. This is she who maintains in their splendor all those who are the enemies of pilgrims. Jas 4:4. Indeed, this is she who has bought off many a man from a pilgrim’s life. She is a great gossip; she is always, both she and her daughters, at one pilgrim’s heels or another’s, now commending, and then promoting the excellences of this life. She is a bold and impudent hussy: she will talk with any man. She always laughs poor pilgrims to scorn, but highly commends the rich. If there is one cunning at getting money in a place, she will speak well of him from house to house. She loves banqueting and feasting very much; she is always at one full table or another. She has given it out in some places that she is a goddess, and therefore some worship her. She has her times and open places of cheating; and she will say and avow it that none can show a good comparable to hers. She promises to dwell with children’s children, if they will only love her and make much of her. She will cast gold out of her purse like dust in some places and to some persons. She loves to be sought after, spoken well of, and to lie in the bosoms of men. She is never weary of commending her goods, and she loves those most who think best of her. She will promise crowns and kingdoms to some, if they will only take her advice; yet many has she brought to the noose, and ten thousand times more to hell.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Standfast]: Oh, said Standfast, what a mercy it is that I resisted her; for where might she have drawn me!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Where? Indeed, none but God knows where. But in general, to be sure, she would have drawn you into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition. 1 Tim 6:9. It was she who set Absalom against his father, and Jeroboam against his master. It was she who persuaded Judas to sell his Lord, and who prevailed with Demas to forsake the godly pilgrim’s life. No one can tell the mischief that she does. She makes strife between rulers and subjects, between parents and children, between neighbor and neighbor, between a man and his wife, between a man and himself, between the flesh and the spirit. So, good Mr. Standfast, be as your name is, and when you have done all, stand.]

At this discourse there was among the pilgrims a mixture of joy and trembling; but at length they broke out and sang,

#quote[“What danger is the Pilgrim in! \
How many are his foes! \
How many ways there are to sin \
No living mortal knows.]

#quote[Some in the ditch are spoiled, yea, can \
Lie tumbling in the mire: \
Some, though they shun the frying-pan \
Do leap into the fire.”]

After this, I beheld until they had come into the land of Beulah, where the sun shines night and day. Here, because they were weary, they gave themselves a while to rest. And because this country was open to pilgrims, and because the orchards and vineyards that were here belonged to the King of the Celestial country, they were therefore free to make use of any of his things. But a little while soon refreshed them here; for the bells rang so, and the trumpets continually sounded so melodiously, that they could not sleep, and yet they received as much refreshment as if they had slept their sleep ever so soundly. Here also all the cry of those who walked the streets was, More pilgrims have come to town! And another would answer, saying, And so many went over the water, and were let in at the golden gates today! They would cry again, There is now a legion of shining ones just come to town, by which we know that there are more pilgrims on the road; for here they come to wait for them, and to comfort them after all their sorrow. Then the pilgrims got up, and walked to and fro. But how their ears were now filled with heavenly noises, and their eyes delighted with celestial visions! In this land they heard nothing, saw nothing, felt nothing, smelled nothing, tasted nothing that was offensive to their stomach or mind; only when they tasted the water of the river over which they were to go, they thought that it tasted a little bitterish to the palate; but it proved sweeter when it was down.

In this place there was a record kept of the names of those who had been pilgrims of old, and a history of all the famous acts that they had done. It was also much talked of here how the river had had its flowings for some, and what ebbings it has had while others have gone over. It has been almost dry for some, while it has overflowed its banks for others.

In this place the children of the town would go into the King’s gardens, and gather posies for the pilgrims, and bring them to them with much affection. Here also grew henna, with spikenard and saffron, calamus and cinnamon, with all the trees of frankincense, myrrh, and aloes, with all the chief spices. With these the pilgrims’ chambers were perfumed while they stayed here; and with these their bodies were anointed, to prepare them to go over the river when the appointed time had come.

Now, while they lay here, and waited for the good hour, there was a noise in the town that a messenger had come from the Celestial City, with a matter of great importance to one Christiana, the wife of Christian the pilgrim. So inquiry was made for her, and the house where she was was found. So the messenger presented her with a letter. The contents were, Hail, good woman; I bring you tidings that the Master calls for you, and expects that you should stand in his presence in clothes of immortality within these ten days.

When he had read this letter to her, he gave her with it a sure token that he was a true messenger, and had come to bid her make haste to be gone. The token was an arrow with a point sharpened with love, let easily into her heart, which by degrees worked so effectually with her that at the time appointed she must be gone.

When Christiana saw that her time had come, and that she was the first of this company who was to go over, she called for Mr. Great-Heart her guide, and told him how matters were. So he told her he was heartily glad of the news, and would have been glad had the messenger come for him. Then she asked him to give advice on how all things should be prepared for her journey. So he told her, saying, Thus and thus it must be, and we who survive will accompany you to the river-side.

Then she called for her children, and gave them her blessing, and told them that she had read with comfort the mark that was set on their foreheads, and was glad to see them with her there, and that they had kept their garments so white. Lastly, she bequeathed to the poor the little she had, and commanded her sons and daughters to be ready for when the messenger should come for them.

When she had spoken these words to her guide and to her children, she called for Mr. Valiant-for-truth, and said to him, Sir, you have in all places shown yourself true-hearted; be faithful unto death, and my King will give you a crown of life. Rev 2:10. I would also entreat you to keep an eye on my children; and if at any time you see them faint, speak comfortingly to them. As for my daughters, my sons’ wives, they have been faithful, and a fulfilling of the promise upon them will be their end. But she gave Mr. Standfast a ring.

Then she called for old Mr. Honest, and said of him, “Here is a true Israelite, in whom there is no deceit!” John 1:47. Then he said, I wish you a fair day when you set out for Mount Zion, and shall be glad to see that you go over the river dry-shod. But she answered, Come wet, come dry, I long to be gone; for however the weather is on my journey, I shall have time enough when I come there to sit down and rest and dry myself.

Then that good man Mr. Ready-to-halt came in to see her. So she said to him, Your travel until now has been with difficulty; but that will make your rest the sweeter. Watch, and be ready; for at an hour when you do not think, the messenger may come.

After him came Mr. Despondency and his daughter Much-afraid, to whom she said, You ought, with thankfulness, forever to remember your deliverance from the hands of Giant Despair, and out of Doubting Castle. The effect of that mercy is that you are brought here in safety. You#super[+] be watchful, and cast away fear; be sober, and hope to the end.

Then she said to Mr. Feeble-mind, You were delivered from the mouth of Giant Slay-good, that you might live in the light of the living, and see your King with comfort. Only I advise you to repent of your aptness to fear and doubt his goodness, before he sends for you; lest you should, when he comes, be forced to stand before him blushing for that fault.

Now the day drew on when Christiana must be gone. So the road was full of people to see her take her journey. But behold, all the banks beyond the river were full of horses and chariots, which had come down from above to accompany her to the city gate. So she came forth, and entered the river, with a wave of farewell to those who followed her. The last words she was heard to say were, I come, Lord, to be with you and bless you! So her children and friends returned to their place, for those who waited for Christiana had carried her out of their sight. So she went and called, and entered in at the gate with all the ceremonies of joy that her husband Christian had entered with before her. At her departure, the children wept. But Mr. Great-Heart and Mr. Valiant played on the well-tuned cymbal and harp for joy. So all departed to their respective places.

In the course of time a messenger came to the town again, and his business was with Mr. Ready-to-halt. So he sought him out, and said, I have come from Him whom you have loved and followed, though on crutches; and my message is to tell you that he expects you at his table to sup with him in his kingdom, the next day after Easter; so prepare yourself for this journey. Then he also gave him a token that he was a true messenger, saying, “I have broken your golden bowl, and loosed your silver cord.” Eccl 12:6.

After this, Mr. Ready-to-halt called for his fellow-pilgrims, and told them, saying, I am sent for, and God will surely visit you also. So he asked Mr. Valiant to make his will. And because he had nothing to bequeath to those who should survive him but his crutches and his good wishes, therefore he said this: These crutches I bequeath to my son who shall tread in my steps, with a hundred warm wishes that he may prove better than I have been.

Then he thanked Mr. Great-Heart for his guidance and kindness, and so made ready for his journey. When he came to the brink of the river, he said, Now I shall have no more need of these crutches, since over there are chariots and horses for me to ride on. The last words he was heard to say were, Welcome life! So he went his way.

After this, Mr. Feeble-mind had news brought to him that the messenger sounded his horn at his chamber door. Then he came in, and told him, saying, I have come to tell you that your Master has need of you, and that in a very little time you must behold his face in brightness. And take this as a token of the truth of my message: “Those watching through windows are darkened.” Eccl 12:3. Then Mr. Feeble-mind called for his friends, and told them what errand had been brought to him, and what token he had received of the truth of the message. Then he said, Since I have nothing to bequeath to anyone, to what purpose should I make a will? As for my feeble mind, that I will leave behind me, for I shall have no need of it in the place where I am going, nor is it worth giving to the poorest pilgrims: so, when I am gone, I desire that you, Mr. Valiant, would bury it in a dunghill. This done, and the day having come on which he was to depart, he entered the river as the rest did. His last words were, Hold out, faith and patience! So he went over to the other side.

When many days had passed away, Mr. Despondency was sent for; for a messenger had come, and brought this message to him: Trembling man! this is to summon you to be ready with the King by the next Lord’s day, to shout for joy for your deliverance from all your doubtings. And, said the messenger, that my message is true, take this for a proof: so he gave him a grasshopper to be a burden to him. Eccl 12:5.

Now Mr. Despondency’s daughter, whose name was Much-afraid, said, when she heard what was done, that she would go with her father. Then Mr. Despondency said to his friends, Myself and my daughter, you know what we have been, and how troublesomely we have behaved ourselves in every company. My will and my daughter’s is that our desponds and slavish fears be received by no man, from the day of our departure, forever; for I know that after my death they will offer themselves to others. For, to be plain with you, they are ghosts which we entertained when we first began to be pilgrims, and could never shake them off afterward; and they will walk about, and seek a welcome from the pilgrims: but for our sakes, shut the doors on them. When the time had come for them to depart, they went up to the brink of the river. The last words of Mr. Despondency were, Farewell, night; welcome, day! His daughter went through the river singing, but none could understand what she said.

Then it came to pass a while after that there was a messenger in the town who inquired for Mr. Honest. So he came to the house where he was, and delivered these lines into his hand: You are commanded to be ready a week from this day, to present yourself before your Lord at his Father’s house. And for a token that my message is true, “All the daughters of song grow faint.” Eccl 12:4. Then Mr. Honest called for his friends, and said to them, I die, but shall make no will. As for my honesty, it shall go with me; let him who comes after be told of this. When the day had come that he was to be gone, he made ready to go over the river. Now the river at that time overflowed its banks in some places; but Mr. Honest, in his lifetime, had spoken to one Good-conscience to meet him there, which he also did, and lent him his hand, and so helped him over. The last words of Mr. Honest were, Grace reigns! So he left the world.

After this it was spread abroad that Mr. Valiant-for-truth was taken with a summons by the same messenger as the other, and had this for a token that the summons was true, “That his pitcher was broken at the fountain.” Eccl 12:6. When he understood it, he called for his friends, and told them of it. Then he said, I am going to my Father’s; and though I have got here with great difficulty, yet now I do not repent of all the trouble I have been at to arrive where I am. My sword I give to him who shall succeed me in my pilgrimage, and my courage and skill to him who can get it. My marks and scars I carry with me, to be a witness for me that I have fought His battles who will now be my rewarder. When the day had come that he must go from here, many accompanied him to the river-side, into which as he went, he said, “Death, where is your sting?” And as he went down deeper, he said, “Grave, where is your victory?” 1 Cor 15:55. So he passed over, and all the trumpets sounded for him on the other side.

Then there came forth a summons for Mr. Standfast. This Mr. Standfast was he whom the rest of the pilgrims found on his knees in the Enchanted Ground. And the messenger brought it to him open in his hands: its contents were that he must prepare for a change of life, for his Master was not willing that he should be so far from him any longer. At this Mr. Standfast fell into deep musing. No, said the messenger, you need not doubt the truth of my message; for here is a token of its truth, “Your wheel is broken at the cistern.” Eccl 12:6. Then he called to him Mr. Great-Heart, who was their guide, and said to him, Sir, although it was not my lot to be much in your good company during the days of my pilgrimage, yet since the time I knew you, you have been profitable to me. When I came from home, I left behind me a wife and five small children; let me entreat you, at your return (for I know that you go and return to your Master’s house, in hopes that you may yet be a guide to more of the holy pilgrims), to send to my family, and let them be told all that has happened and shall happen to me. Tell them moreover of my happy arrival at this place, and of the present and late blessed condition I am in. Tell them also of Christian and Christiana his wife, and how she and her children came after her husband. Tell them also what a happy end she made, and where she has gone. I have little or nothing to send to my family, unless it is prayers and tears for them; of which it will be enough that you tell them, if perhaps they may prevail. When Mr. Standfast had thus set things in order, and the time had come for him to hasten away, he also went down to the river. Now there was a great calm at that time in the river; so Mr. Standfast, when he was about half-way in, stood a while, and talked with his companions who had attended him there. And he said, This river has been a terror to many; yes, the thoughts of it have also often frightened me; but now I think I stand easy; my foot is fixed upon that on which the feet of the priests who bore the ark of the covenant stood while Israel went over Jordan. Josh 3:17. The waters indeed are bitter to the palate, and cold to the stomach; yet the thoughts of what I am going to, and of the convoy that waits for me on the other side, lie like a glowing coal at my heart. I see myself now at the end of my journey; my toilsome days are ended. I am going to see that head which was crowned with thorns, and that face which was spit upon for me. I have formerly lived by hearsay and faith; but now I go where I shall live by sight, and shall be with him in whose company I delight myself. I have loved to hear my Lord spoken of; and wherever I have seen the print of his shoe in the earth, there I have longed to set my foot too. His name has been to me as a civet-box; yes, sweeter than all perfumes. His voice to me has been most sweet, and his countenance I have desired more than those who have most desired the light of the sun. His words I used to gather for my food, and for antidotes against my faintings. He has held me, and has kept me from my iniquities; yes, my steps he has strengthened in his way.

Now, while he was speaking like this, his countenance changed; his strong man bowed under him: and after he had said, Take me, for I come to you, he ceased to be seen by them.

But glorious it was to see how the open region was filled with horses and chariots, with trumpeters and pipers, with singers and players on stringed instruments, to welcome the pilgrims as they went up, and followed one another in at the beautiful gate of the city.

As for Christiana’s children, the four boys that Christiana brought, with their wives and children, I did not stay where I was till they had gone over. Also, since I came away, I heard someone say that they were still alive, and so would be for the increase of the church in that place where they were, for a time.

Should it be my lot to go that way again, I may give those who desire it an account of what I am silent about here: meanwhile I bid my reader

#align(center)[FAREWELL.]

#align(center)[THE END.]
